\begin{shaded}

\section*{Summary} A retrospective analysis of the Pember Fan monitoring data indicates that the species composition of the fan is changing. Grasses (both exotic and indigenous) are increasing in areal coverage while forbs are diminishing. At the same time the amount of bare ground is shrinking. There are also indications the fan is becoming more depauperate over time, with indigenous biodiversity increasing with perpendicular distance from farming operations. \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical), an indigenous forb restricted to the southern half of the fan is disappearing quickly, and extinction appears eminent if its current trajectory continues.  

Currently, there is a proposal to increase stock grazing to increase the rate of disturbance to preserve this species from being usurped by grasses. However, given the current management constraints, an experimental grazing approach discussed during ameeting was considered impractical. However, it was identified that localised experimental plots could also be established on the fan, outside existing transects, to test vegetation control (e.g. mowing) around known \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} plants, providing replicated, randomly assigned treatments and causal evidence to inform management without disrupting farm activities. At the same time the original monitoring regime could be maintained with utility in comparing response trajectories in the planned conservation covenant (cattle grazing) and recreation reserve (sheep grazing).


\end{shaded}


\section*{Background}

The Pember Fan is an alluvial outwash surface in Lees Valley, North Canterbury, formed from gravels deposited where the Ashley/Rakahuri River emerges into the valley. Much of the area is classified as a 'threatened land environment’. The fan supports a grassland which includes several nationally threatened and at-risk plants, most notably the nationally critical Lees Valley daisy (\emph{Brachyscome pinnata}), whose only known natural population occurs there and whose seed, unlike most members of the Asteraceae family, are not wind dispersed. Other threatened species known to inhabit the fan include \emph{Gingidia enysii}, \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} and \emph{Raoulia monroi}\footnote{Not present in the transect data}.

In 2019 the Department of Conservation established monitoring programme based on 30 plots (each with 50 subplots) to monitor vegetative changes in this ecosystem through time. The underlying purpose of this monitoring programme was to inform management. Key metrics focused on changes in:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Indigenous biodiversity
  \item Plant composition (especially threatened species) 
  \item Ground cover
\end{itemize}

The 30 transects which make up the monitoring programe consist of two types: (1)  ten-closely spaced transects restricted to a recreational reserve, and (2) twenty more widely-spaced transects found across a planned conservation covenant.

\section*{Purpose of current analysis}

It is hypothesised that the current grazing regime is insufficient to regulate the crowding out of native herbs by exotic grasses at the site. Going forward it was agreed that a two stage analysis of the data would take place:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stage 1:} Would consist of a series of retrospective analyses to understand the trajectories at the site in respect of the three metrics previously identified. The deliverables were to include a short report that would inform a discussion with Richard Clayton and Ellen Cieraad regarding on the direction of the next stage.
  \item \textbf{Stage 2:} Based on the decisions undertaken at the end of Stage 1, the signals present in the data, and operational constraints, a series of statistical tools would be employed to identify the design parameters required to detect the effectiveness future management. On 24 October 2016 a video meeting was held in order to inform management conversations with the Lees Valley Station for the forthcoming season\footnote{Sandy Yong, Ellen Cieraad, and Nathan Whitmore were attendance.}. The management recommendations were:
  
  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Graze the whole of fan with cattle this spring to start to 'reset' the fan to reduce the thatch this spring. 
  \item Split the fan in half along existing fencing lines to have more control over evenness of grazing over the whole of the fan (noting that \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears restricted to the Southern portion of the fan, and that open grazing could result in this area being under grazed).
  \item Include a specific number of cattle in the recreation reserve to similarly drive down the thatch to be followed by sheep grazing in the autumn.
\end{itemize}
  
In light of these management constraints, an experimental approach in terms of grazing was assessed as impractical. We can however, maintain the original monitoring regime to compare the trajectories of the responses in the planned conservation covenant (cattle grazing) and recreation reserve (sheep grazing). Furthermore, it was identified that localised experimental plots could be established in the fan but outside the current transect lines to trial the effectiveness of vegetation control (e.g. mowing) around known \emph{Brachyscome pinnata} plants. Such an approach could yield causal information informing fan management without impinging on farm activities, with the benefit of random treatment assignment, replication, and a nearby seed source to enable a potential positive response.
  


\end{itemize}




\section*{Exploratory analysis}

The analysis began with some simple proof of concept analyses involving indigenous biodiversity and sequentially introduced complexity. Such an approach ensured that any mistakes or misunderstanding in purpose, or underlying data were quickly identified and corrected. Furthermore, such an approach allowed us to identify any design and convergence issues which would likely affect experimental designs.


\subsection*{Indigenous biodiversity}

Initial exploratory modelling focused on indigenous biodiversity due to its high tangibility and suggested the area is becoming more depauperate over (see Appendix). When more complex analyses were undertaken incorporating spatial elements it was revealed the that while the transects across the convenant area (CA plots) were well dispersed across the fan, the RR plots were tightly clustered within a reserve found on the southern edge of the fan but within the wider CA extent. Such tight spatial clustering typically presents a problem in terms of sampling bias and raises issues of spatial auto-correlation. Initial models\footnote{Upon suggestion from Ellen Cieraad I implemented a series of models which examined whether the response variable was associated with a decaying distance relationship with neighbouring observations i.e. via a \emph{exp(pos + 0 $\mid$ spatial\_group)}.} failed to converge leading me to suspect there might be a directional gradient underpinning the random errors. Formal tests of the random errors via Moran's I test revealed that there was some form of spatial auto-correlation. 

Plotting the random errors associated with the top-ranked model suggested a spatial relationship running perpendicular to the Ashley River (Figure \ref{fig:spatial.signal}). I then tested Richard Clayton's suggestion that the relationship might be better described by the relationship with the fence line. Indeed, modelling with both terms revealed that there was likely some gradient influencing the biodiversity response associated with distance from the fence line (Figure \ref{fig:signal.corrected}). Inclusion of this term resulted in the random errors associated with plot diminishing substantially, and a new top-ranked model being established. 

The predictions from the new top-ranked model suggest that samples found closer to the fence line have higher indigenous biodiversity but more likely to loose indigenous species at a faster rate than those located further away (Figure \ref{fig:distance.fence}).


