\subsection*{Floral Species Composition}

Floral species composition was analysed graphically by was by transforming cover classes into approximate proportions at the subplot level. Collectively, the species composition were dominated by exotic plants, although over time indigenous plants were gradually representing a greater proportion of the species (Figure \ref{fig:TaxonBiostatus}).

Across the transects there was a trend towards increasing dominance of species with gramminoid (grass-like) growth forms and away from forbs (Figure \ref{fig:TaxonGrowthForm}). This appears to be a generalised trend, with a similar pattern occurring on transects inside the DOC reserve and the wider covenant area  (Figure \ref{fig:bytransforbs} \& \ref{fig:bytransgrass}). It is not only exotic grasses which appear to be increasingly dominating the Pember Fan but indigenous grasses as well, while at the same time the coverage by indigenous forbs is declining (Figure \ref{fig:bytransforbs}). While there are some differences between transect types the composition of the grass species seems to be relatively stable  dominated by \emph{Agrostis capillaris}, 
\emph{Anthoxanthum odoratum} and \emph{Rytidosperma clavatum}. Both transect types document the gradual disappearance of \emph{Vulpia bromoides}, a species often associated with disturbance (Figure  \ref{fig:bytransgrass}). In contrast the forb community appears to be completely dominated by the hawkweed \emph{Pilosella officinarum} (Figure \ref{fig:top8forb}).

