\subsection*{Key species}

The three plant species present on the fan with the highest threat classification were singled out for modelling\footnote{I note that while the fan-leaved mat daisy \emph{Raoulia monroi} (Nationally Vulnerable) has been reported as being present in the valley it was not recorded in the current dataset.}. These were:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Lees Valley Daisy,\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} (Nationally Critical)
  \item Dryland sow thistle \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} (Nationally Vulnerable) 
    \item Limestone aniseed \emph{Gingidia enysii} (Nationally Endangered) 

\end{itemize}

As plant cover was estimated using ordered cover class categories. Cumulative link models with random effects were used to estimate 


\emph{Brachyscome pinnata} appears to be constrained to the southern portion of the fan and appears to be fast disappearing (Figure \ref{fig:brapin}). Cuumulative modelling revealed that the top-ranked model with unianmous support was \emph{as.factor(Year) + scale(fence.dist) + (1 | Plot) + (1 | SubplotID} which revealed cover for this species is declining, especially in the last three years and there is a non-significant trend of higher coverage closer to the fence. 

There is no evidence that \emph{Sonchus novae-zelandiae} is declining but rather it's presence maybe associated with a gradient extending from the fence (Figure \ref{fig:sonnov}; top-ranked model \emph{scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.40). However, the second ranked model was a null model thereby suggesting there is effectively no detectable pattern whatsoever (\emph{1 + (1 $\mid$ Plot)}, AICc weight = 0.33).




