\subsection*{Notes on bare-ground hurdle modelling}

A hurdle model was used initially to assess changes in bare ground cover (logistic regression to assess presence-absence followed by a beta binomial regression to determine proportion of bare ground). The presence of bare ground was associated with year (top ranked model: \emph{as.factor (Year) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.73) with bare ground diminishing over time (from an average of 18.2\% per subplot in 2018 to 2.6\% in 2026).

Modelling suggest that the proportion of bare ground increased with proximity to the fence but was declining over time (top ranked mode: \emph{Year + scale(fence.dist) + (1 $\mid$ Plot/Subplot)}, AICc weight = 0.43). However this beta regression model had multimodal residuals, suggesting the existence of a potential hidden process (likely as an artifact of the proportions being derived from cover classes - as a consequence it was determined that cumulative link models wee more trustworthy.) 

